        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "cf218708-b7a8-557f-a303-022a097c9eef",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Places: New York State, Quebec, Catskill Mountains, Lake Huron, New England, Laurentia, North America\n- Events: Taconic orogeny, Late Ordovician volcanic eruption\n- Geologic formations: Queenston Delta, Deicke and Millbrig bentonite layers, K-bentonite\n\nFacts:\n- The Taconic orogeny was a mountain-building period that ended 440 million years ago and affected most of modern-day New England.\n- During the Late Ordovician on Laurentia, the craton of North America, a volcanic eruption during the Taconic orogeny formed the Deicke and Millbrig bentonite layers, including the potassium bentonite layer called K-bentonite.\n- The Queenston Delta wedge is thickest in a band running from New York State to Quebec and extends from the Catskill Mountains to Lake Huron.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
